    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate prioritizing explosive matchups over raw season averages. Our top-tier offensive output is anchored by **Mickey Moniak** (75.97) and **James Wood** (75.59), who represent our strongest plays of the day. **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% platoon edge against Lowder, while **James Wood** exploits a favorable matchup against Ginn, buoyed by an elite 0.437 xwOBA and an aggressive #1 spot in the lineup. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.71) round out the core, with **Vinnie Pasquantino** drawing a juicy matchup against Canning and **Tyler Soderstrom** enjoying a 10% park factor bump. **Corbin Carroll** remains a core starter (69.43); while we acknowledge the -4% efficiency tax due to his elite 55% SB threat status against a high-hold pitcher, his speed remains a non-negotiable component of our Roto strategy.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley** (52.16), who was edged out of the utility spot by the superior platoon efficiency of **Vinnie Pasquantino**. While **Austin Riley** has favorable BvP indicators against Gore, the optimizer favored **Vinnie Pasquantino**\u2019s raw matchup projections against Canning. We have also scrubbed the lineup of any risk regarding **Jose Caballero**, as his game has been postponed; we will keep an eye on his status for future rotation. Conversely, **Travis Bazzana** failed to meet our \"Zebras Floor\" of 40.0, largely due to a harsh 19% penalty against Montgomery\u2019s skill set and an unfavorable L/L platoon alignment. As we navigate the schedule, we are exercising caution with **Cole Young**, whose 40.05 score is dangerously close to our bench threshold; should his production dip further, he will be the first candidate to be replaced by bench depth like **Giancarlo Stanton** or **Max Muncy** depending on daily platoon availability.",
